.TH GXWI-EVENT-VIEWER 1 "2026-10-06" "Peios"
.SH NAME
gxwi-event-viewer \- read this machine's events, metrics and logs, as they happen, on a GXWI desktop
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gxwi-event-viewer
.RB [ \-\-events " | " \-\-metrics " | " \-\-settings ]
.br
.B gxwi-event-viewer \-\-logs
.I ORIGIN
.SH DESCRIPTION
Event Viewer shows what eventd has recorded on this machine: the events of
its kernel and programs, the metrics programs publish, and the logs of its
services. It has four tabs: Events, Metrics, Logs and Settings. Events and
logs are listed newest first, and new ones are added at the top as they are
recorded; Pause keeps the list still, and Resume carries on. The list starts
with the newest 200; Show older adds the 200 before them, and a window holds
at most 1,000. Events narrow by type and source, logs by where they came
from, by text, and to errors only. Picking a row shows all of it beside the
list.
.PP
Metrics lists every metric the person may read, and charts them on
dashboards they arrange. A person's dashboards are their own, kept in the
registry at CurrentUser\eSoftware\egxwi-event-viewer; until they make one,
they are shown eventd's health.
.PP
It asks eventd, on its query socket, as the person looking, so it shows
exactly what they may read. eventd leaves out what they may not read
without saying so; Event Viewer reads eventd's read policy as them and says
what is kept from them, rather than showing an empty list as if there were
nothing.
.PP
Settings shows every value under Machine\eSystem\eeventd, described as
eventd's own regman page describes it, and who may read what. Anyone may
look. Only those allowed to change eventd's settings may change them, as
shipped Administrators; everyone else sees them read-only, with a line
saying why. Each pattern of the read policy opens in the permissions editor
to be changed; a pattern may be added, and removed after asking, but the *
patterns cannot be removed.
.PP
Without a desktop to open on, it says so and ends: on a terminal, evctl does
what this does.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-events
Open on the events. This is what it does with no option.
.TP
.B \-\-metrics
Open on the person's dashboards of metrics.
.TP
.B \-\-settings
Open on eventd's settings and read policy.
.TP
.BI \-\-logs " ORIGIN"
Open on what
.I ORIGIN
has logged: a service, with its hooks and health checks, or whatever else
logs under that name. Services Manager's Logs... opens it so.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/apps/dev.peios.gxwi-event-viewer.toml
Its application declaration, listed in a launcher as Event Viewer.
.TP
.I /run/eventd/query.sock
eventd's query socket, which it asks.
.TP
.I /usr/share/regman/eventd.regman
eventd's regman page, from which the Settings tab takes what each value
means, its default and its range.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR evctl (1),
.BR gxwi-sd-editor (1).
.PP
Event Viewer, at learn.peios.org.
